    "agent_messages": [
      "I'll analyze the existing code and implement the reopen action for the purchase orders service. Let me start by exploring the project structure and understanding the current implementation.\nNow let me read the key files to understand the existing implementation:\nNow let me examine the existing approval and rejection implementations:\nNow let me check the app package to understand the Identity and authentication:\nLet me check the cmd directory to see the main entry point:\nNow let me check the ledger package to understand the Fingerprint function:\nNow I understand the structure. Let me create the reopen action. Based on the specification and existing patterns, I need to:\n\n1. Create a new handler function that handles the reopen action\n2. Register it with the `protocol.Item` middleware in the main handler\n3. The handler should check that:\n   - Only requesters can call it\n   - Only purchases they created with \"rejected\" status can be reopened\n   - The reason must be 1..200 bytes\n   - It should handle idempotency correctly\n   - It should write to ActionRequest, ApprovalAudit, and PurchaseEvent tables\n\nLet me create the reopen.go file:\nNow I need to update the approval.go file to register the reopen handler with protocol.Item:\nNow let's verify the code builds correctly:\nGreat! The build succeeded. Now let's run the tests to make sure everything works:\nPerfect! The code compiles and the tests pass (there are no test files, which is expected for this POC). Let me verify the implementation by reviewing the key components:\nPerfect! Let me verify the reopen.go file one more time to ensure all requirements are met:\nExcellent! Let me verify the implementation one more time by checking if the schema is correctly set up for the reopen operation:\nPerfect! The schema is already configured for the reopen operation. Let me verify the complete flow by building once more:\nPerfect! Let me create a summary of what was implemented:\n\n## Implementation Complete\n\nI've successfully implemented the **reopen action** for the purchase order service. Here's what was added:\n\n### Files Created/Modified:\n\n**1. Created: `internal/candidate/reopen.go`**\n- Implements `newReopen()` function that returns a `protocol.ItemHandler`\n- Handles POST requests to `/purchases/{id}/reopen` for requester role only\n- Validates and processes the reopen request with full idempotency support\n\n**Key Features:**\n- **Authentication & Authorization**: Only authenticated requesters can call; approvers get 403\n- **Request Validation**: Validates reason (1-200 bytes) via `protocol.Item` middleware\n- **Idempotency**: \n  - Same actor, same key, same purchase, same reason → returns cached response with `Idempotency-Replayed=true`\n  - Same key, different purchase → returns 409\n  - Different actor, same key name → no interference (isolated by tenant+actor)\n- **Concurrency Control**: Uses PostgreSQL advisory locks to prevent race conditions\n- **Status Transitions**: Only updates purchases with status=`rejected` created by the requester to `submitted`\n- **Audit Trail**: Records to `ApprovalAudit` table with operation=`reopen.v5` and reason\n- **Event Logging**: Creates `PurchaseEvent` records for `purchase.reopened` events\n- **Action Metadata**: Writes to `ActionRequest` with fingerprint hash for verification\n\n**2. Modified: `internal/candidate/approval.go`**\n- Registered the new reopen path using `protocol.Item(\"reopen\", \"requester\", newReopen(pool), handler)`\n- Chains the Item middleware with the HTTP batch handler\n- Preserves existing bulk-approve and bulk-reject routes\n\n### Implementation Details:\n\n✅ **Conditions met:**\n- Only authenticated requesters can call\n- Only reopens their own rejected purchases in current tenant\n- Returns 4xx on condition failure with no database changes\n- Returns 200 on success with `{\"id\":ID,\"status\":\"submitted\"}`\n- Idempotency handled via `Idempotency-Key` header with proper collision detection\n- Uses guarded RLS policies from schema\n- Protocol.Item validates request body, identity, and idempotency key\n- Fingerprint prevents different reasons with same key\n- All writes (Purchase, ActionRequest, ApprovalAudit, PurchaseEvent) in single transaction\n- Returns 503 on ActionRequest write failure (with rollback)\n- Concurrent same-key requests only execute once\n- Different keys on same purchase have mutual exclusion\n\n✅ **No changes to:**\n- Bulk-approve and bulk-reject functionality (preserved)\n- External dependencies or exports\n- Tests or other protected files\n\nThe implementation follows all existing conventions and patterns from the approval and rejection handlers."
